NAME
====

**colander** - make Atom and RSS feeds from web pages and other feeds

SYNOPSIS
========

**colander** [**-r** _dir_ | **--rules-dir** _dir_] [**-o** _dir_ | **--output-dir** _dir_]
         [**-O** | **--stdout**] [**-i** _name_ | **--include** _name_]...

**colander** [**-r** _dir_] [**-o** _dir_] [**-O**] [**-e** _name_ | **--exclude** _name_]...

**colander** [**-v** | **--version**]

**colander** [**-h** | **--help**]

DESCRIPTION
===========

**colander** makes a feed for each rules file in the rules directory. A rules
file names a page, which can be HTML or XML (such as another feed), and says
what to extract from it, how to change what it extracts and which entries to
keep. **colander** fetches the page, extracts the feed's information and
entries, and writes the feed as Atom or as RSS for podcasts.

The feed for ``<rules-dir>/<name>.wattle`` is written to
``<output-dir>/<name>.xml``. It is written to a temporary file in the output
directory and renamed into place, so a reader never sees part of a feed. The
output directory is created if it does not exist.

Each feed is made in turn. A feed that fails, because its page cannot be
fetched, its status is not 2xx or its rules file has an error, is reported
on standard error as ``colander: <name>: <reason>`` and its file is left as
it was. The other feeds are still made. A feed with no entries is written,
with a warning, since that usually means that its page has changed.

**colander** prints nothing else, so it can be run from cron.

OPTIONS
=======

- **-r** _dir_ | **--rules-dir** _dir_:
  The directory of rules files. This defaults to ``rules``.

- **-o** _dir_ | **--output-dir** _dir_:
  The directory to write feeds to. This defaults to ``outputs``.

- **-O** | **--stdout**:
  Write each feed to standard output instead of to a file.

- **-i** _name_ | **--include** _name_:
  Make only the feed for the rules file _name_ (without ``.wattle``). Can be
  given more than once; the feeds are made in the order given.

- **-e** _name_ | **--exclude** _name_:
  Make every feed except the one for _name_. Can be given more than once.
  It cannot be used with **--include**.

- **-v** | **--version**:
  Print the version and exit.

- **-h** | **--help**:
  Print a help message and exit.

A name given with **--include** or **--exclude** that has no rules file is an
error, and no feed is made.

RULES FILES
===========

A rules file is a Wattle program that defines ``feed``, a map. It is
compiled in an environment of its own, so it can define other bindings for
its own use, and it can use Wattle's standard library. Of **colander**'s own
functions it has ``extract``, ``header`` and those that begin with
``markup/``, ``date/``, ``select/`` and ``template/``.

A rules file is code: **colander** runs it. Use rules files that you have
written or read.

The keys of ``feed`` are:

- ``:url``:
  The page to fetch, a string that begins with ``http://`` or ``https://``.
  Required.

- ``:format``:
  How to read the page, ``:html`` (the default) or ``:xml``.

- ``:template``:
  How to write the feed: ``:atom`` (the default), ``:podcast``, or a
  function. See `<TEMPLATES>`.

- ``:info``:
  The feed's own elements, as a vector of ``[name value]`` pairs.

- ``:entries``:
  An ``extract`` rule that selects each entry's element in the page.

- ``:entry``:
  Each entry's elements, as a vector of ``[name value]`` pairs.

- ``:keep?``:
  A function of an entry that returns true to keep it.

- ``:transform``:
  A function from an entry to the entry to write.

- ``:headers``:
  A map of request headers to send, such as ``{"User-Agent" "..."}``.

- ``:timeout``:
  The seconds to wait for the page. This defaults to 30.

A name is a keyword, such as ``:title`` or ``:itunes:image``; it is the name
of the element written. A value is a string, an ``extract`` rule or a
``header`` rule. The elements are written in the order of the pairs.

With ``:entries``, the rules in ``:entry`` select within each entry's
element. Without it, each rule in ``:entry`` selects every match in the page,
and the first match of each goes to the first entry, the second to the
second, and so on.

``:keep?`` and ``:transform`` are given each entry as a map of names to
strings, such as ``{:title "..." :link "..."}``. ``:keep?`` is called first.
A transform can change, add or remove values. Values that it adds are
written after the others, in order of name.

EXTRACTING
==========

``(extract selector & options)`` returns a rule that takes a value from the
first element that _selector_ matches. The selector is searched for in the
entry's element, or in the whole page for a rule in ``:info`` or with
``:absolute``. An empty selector is the entry's element itself. The options
are:

- ``:content``:
  What to take from the element: ``:text`` (the default, its text with the
  surrounding whitespace removed), ``:inner-html`` (its contents as markup),
  or ``:html`` or ``:xml`` (the element itself as markup, such as an
  ``enclosure``).

- ``:attr``:
  The name of an attribute to take instead, such as ``"href"``.

- ``:absolute``:
  When true, search the whole page rather than the entry's element.

- ``:process``:
  A function of the value and the rule that returns a string.

- ``:prefix``, ``:suffix``:
  Strings to add before and after the value.

- ``:type``:
  ``:datetime`` reads the value as a date and writes it in the form of
  RFC 3339 (see `<DATES>`), or as an empty string if it is not a date.
  ``:cdata`` writes the value in a CDATA section, for a value that is HTML.

The steps are applied in that order: content, process, prefix and suffix,
type. A rule that matches nothing gives an empty string, without its prefix
or suffix.

``(header name & options)`` returns a rule that takes the value of the
response header _name_, in any case, such as ``"Last-Modified"``. It takes
``:process``, ``:prefix``, ``:suffix`` and ``:type``.

SELECTORS
=========

Selectors are a subset of CSS: type selectors (``div``, ``*``), ``.class``,
``#id``, ``[attr]``, ``[attr=v]``, ``[attr~=v]``, ``[attr^=v]``,
``[attr$=v]`` and ``[attr*=v]``, the descendant combinator (a space) and the
child combinator (``>``), and lists separated by ``,``. ``ns|type`` matches
the element ``ns:type`` in XML, such as ``itunes|image``. Any other selector
is an error.

HTML is read leniently, and tag and attribute names are lowercased, so write
HTML selectors in lower case. XML names are kept as written.

TEMPLATES
=========

Each value is written according to its rule: escaped, in a CDATA section
(``:type :cdata``) or as it is (``:content :html`` or ``:xml``).

``:atom`` writes an Atom feed with the ``:info`` elements and an ``entry``
element for each entry. ``:author`` is written as
``<author><name>...</name></author>`` and ``:link`` as
``<link href="..." rel="alternate" />``. A CDATA value in ``title``,
``subtitle``, ``summary``, ``content`` or ``rights`` is marked
``type="html"``.

``:podcast`` writes an RSS 2.0 feed with the iTunes namespace: a ``channel``
with the ``:info`` elements and an ``item`` for each entry.
``:itunes:category`` is written as ``<itunes:category text="..."/>``,
``:itunes:image`` as ``<itunes:image href="..."/>`` and ``:guid`` with
``isPermaLink="false"``.

A function given as ``:template`` is called with a map of ``:info`` and
``:entries``. ``:info`` is a vector of ``[name value mode]`` triples, where
mode is ``:text``, ``:cdata`` or ``:markup``, and ``:entries`` is a vector of
such vectors. It returns the feed as a string.

DATES
=====

``:type :datetime`` reads dates written as ``2026-11-23`` or
``2026-11-23T08:00:00+09:00``, ``Mon, 23 Nov 2026 08:00:00 +0900`` (or with a
zone such as ``GMT``), ``November 23, 2026``, ``23 November 2026`` and
``11/23/2026``. Months and days of the week can be written in full or in
three letters. A date with an offset keeps it; a date without one is in the
local time zone, and a date without a time is midnight.

FETCHING
========

**colander** sends a GET request with the User-Agent
``Colander/<version> (+https://github.com/pyrmont/colander)``, which
``:headers`` can replace. It follows up to five redirects, accepts gzip and
deflate, and reads at most 16 MiB of a response. It uses the system's
certificates for HTTPS.

EXIT STATUS
===========

**colander** exits with 0 if every feed was made, and with 1 if any feed
failed, its options are invalid or the rules directory cannot be read.

EXAMPLES
========

A rules file, ``rules/essays.wattle``, for a page that lists essays:

````
(def- url "https://example.org/essays.html")

(def- link (extract "a" :attr "href" :prefix "https://example.org"))

(def feed
  {:url url
   :info [[:id url]
          [:updated (header "Last-Modified" :type :datetime)]
          [:title "Essays"]
          [:author "A. Writer"]]
   :entries (extract "ul.posts > li")
   :entry [[:id link]
           [:title (extract "a")]
           [:link link]]})
````

A rules file that filters another feed and changes its titles:

````
(def feed
  {:url "https://example.org/podcast.xml"
   :format :xml
   :template :podcast
   :info [[:title "One Segment"]
          [:link "https://example.org/"]]
   :entries (extract "item")
   :entry [[:title (extract "title")]
           [:description (extract "description" :type :cdata)]
           [:enclosure (extract "enclosure" :content :xml)]
           [:guid (extract "guid")]]
   :keep? (fn [e] (string/find "Segment" (e :title)))
   :transform (fn [e]
                (assoc e :title (string/replace-all " | Show" "" (e :title))))})
````

Make every feed in ``rules`` and write them to ``/var/www/feeds``:

````
$ colander -o /var/www/feeds
````

Run every two hours from cron:

````
0 */2 * * * cd /srv/feeds && $HOME/bin/colander -o public
````

Check one feed without writing a file:

````
$ colander -O -i essays
````
